# Beat: guided walk through bob/logfile against alice/app.desc.
#
# Invocation:
#   protolens --descriptor-set alice/app.desc bob/logfile \
#       --script beats/app.desc
#
# The root type is not in alice/app.desc — it is Bob's own log envelope,
# which reproto recovered no schema for.  The session opens untyped.
# This beat names the structure by hand, enables heat cues, and follows
# the cues as far as alice/app.desc can take them.
#
# Re-pin (must print nothing):
#   protolens --descriptor-set alice/app.desc bob/logfile \
#       --script beats/app.desc script \
#     | grep 'error:'

title: The logfile, app.desc

steps:
  - text: |
      This is bob/logfile opened against alice/app.desc.

      This is a larger protobuf than the capture.
      The root type is unknown — no schema in alice/app.desc
      matches the envelope.  What we see is raw field numbers.

      Press space to step.

  - text: |
      Let's fold the top level nodes to get a clearer view.
      Notice the annotation on the 4th node: TRUNCATED_MESSAGE

      The process was probably killed in flight and the logfile misses 1024 bytes.
      This explains why protoc was unable to parse it.

      Press space to step.

    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /
    select: true
    search: TRUNCATED_MESSAGE

  - text: |
      Open to depth 2.

      The top-level fields each contain a repeated submessage.  These
      look like log entries — one per RPC call.

      Press space to step.

    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: "[{]  #@ message"

  - text: |
      Let's name the structure.  The command below is prefilled.

      It declares a new synthetic type "Entry", marks field 1 as
      repeated and gives it the field name "entries".
      
      Press Enter to commit.

    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: "[{]  #@ message"
    command: override /:1 --as-new Entry --card repeated --field-name entries

  - text: |
      To go futher, let's enable protolens heat cues.
      Protolens will tell us where it recognizes nodes with known types.
      They will be lightened up.

      Press i to enable heat cues.

    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: "[{]  #@ repeated Entry"

  - text: |
      /:1:5 is bright green.

      Hover over the heat cue beside /:1:5.  Notice it proposes two matching types
      with identical scores.
      Let's pick corresponds to
      /1/4 — same bytes.  This is a
      google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextRequest from the googleapis.

      Three commands follow.  Announce each one before pressing Enter.

    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/4
    select: true

  - text: |
      Commit the type for /1/4 — the SearchText request.

    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 1", "/1 Z"]
    node: /1/4
    command: override /1/4 --as google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextRequest

  - text: |
      And /1/5 — the SearchText response alongside it.

    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 1", "/1 Z"]
    node: /1/5
    command: override /1/5 --as google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextResponse

  - text: |
      Add a field name to /1/2 — the method line that names the call.

    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 1", "/1 Z"]
    node: /1/2
    command: override /1/2 --field-name rpc

  - text: |
      Now /1/1.

      This looks like a timestamp — two integer fields, which is the
      shape of google.protobuf.Timestamp (seconds + nanos).  The
      command is prefilled.

    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 1", "/1 Z"]
    node: /1/1
    command: override /1/1 --as google.protobuf.Timestamp --field-name timestamp

  - text: |
      /3/6 and /3/7 are dim.

      Hover over their heat cues.  No candidate in alice/app.desc
      scores well enough to light them.  They may use types from a
      schema reproto did not recover — or from a service this descriptor
      set was never built against.

      The full googleapis descriptor set may do better.  We will try
      that next.

    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 1", "/1 Z"]
    node: /3/6

  - text: |
      Before we leave, save our overrides.

      Press Enter to write them to alice/overrides.  The next session
      will load them with --load-overrides.

    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /
    command: save-overrides alice/overrides

  - text: |
      Press Enter to quit.

    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /
    command: quit
